<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/15th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Vaslui</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1475">1475</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Battle of Podul Înalt</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Vaslui was fought on 10 January 1475 at Podul Înalt near Vaslui, in Moldavia. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stephen-the-great">Stephen the Great</a>, with a much smaller army, destroyed an Ottoman force under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadim-suleiman-pasha">Hadım Suleiman Pasha</a> in fog and marshy ground, one of the greatest Christian victories over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> of the century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-vaslui.jpg" alt="Battle of Vaslui" width="320" height="293" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anonimu at English Wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vaslui_15cMD.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stephen-the-great">Stephen III of Moldavia</a> and Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a> quarrelled over southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bessarabia">Bessarabia</a> and its ports of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kiliya">Chilia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bilhorod-dnistrovskyi">Akkerman</a>, essential to Moldavian trade on the route from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feodosia">Caffa</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suceava">Suceava</a> to Lwow. Stephen took Chilia in 1465 and, after beating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matthias-corvinus">Matthias Corvinus</a> at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baia-romania">Baia</a> in 1467, tried to free Wallachia from Ottoman control, fighting Radu the Handsome and backing rival claimants to its throne. In 1473 he stopped paying tribute. He refused Mehmed's ultimatum to give up Chilia and come to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, and in November 1474 asked the Pope for help.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Mehmed sent Suleiman Pasha from the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shkoder">Shkodër</a> with an army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janissaries">Janissaries</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sipahi">sipahis</a>, akinci and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a> cavalry, joined in Wallachia by 17,000 men under Basarab Laiotă. Stephen's army, reinforced by 1,800 Hungarians, 2,000 Polish horsemen and 5,000 Székely soldiers, included some 30,000 peasants of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-heathen-army">Great Army</a>. As the invaders entered Moldavia in December 1474, Stephen burned the land, poisoned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">wells</a> and sent people and livestock into the mountains.</p><p>He chose to fight at the wooden bridge of Podul Înalt over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barlad-river">Bârlad River</a>, in a marshy valley ringed by wooded hills. On the foggy morning of 10 January, musicians in the valley drew the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> forward into fire from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> and handgunners on three sides, and the bridge collapsed under their cavalry. Then Stephen attacked from all sides while buglers behind the Ottoman lines spread confusion. Suleiman lost control and retreated, pursued for three days to Obluciţa. Jan Długosz writes that the prisoners were impaled.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-sixtus-iv">Pope Sixtus IV</a> named Stephen Athleta Christi but sent no help, and King Casimir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> did nothing. Mehmed invaded in person in 1476 and defeated Stephen at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-valea-alba">Battle of Valea Albă</a>, but failed to take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suceava">Suceava</a> and Neamț. In 1484 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayezid-ii">Bayezid II</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kiliya">Chilia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bilhorod-dnistrovskyi">Cetatea Albă</a>. Stephen built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voronet-monastery">Voroneț Monastery</a> in 1488 to commemorate the victory.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Vaslui” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He took Chilia in 1465, repulsed the Hungarian king Matthias Corvinus at Baia in 1467, and on 10 January 1475 destroyed a much larger Ottoman army under Hadım Suleiman Pasha at the <b>Battle of Vaslui</b>, fought in fog near the Bârlad River.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-moldavia">Principality of Moldavia · Military</a></li></ul></section>
